<h2>The contract and the cancelled order</h2>
<p>The purchase order of 2 March 2019 was for structural material for a transmission project, priced at roughly Rs 27.72 crore. Two clauses did the work in the eventual dispute. Clause 16 tied the 10% advance to an advance bank guarantee and a proforma invoice; Clause 38 barred manufacturing without written manufacturing clearance. Skipper furnished a guarantee of Rs 2.35 crore in June 2019; Flowmore never paid the advance and cancelled the order in July 2019. Skipper obtained interim relief under Section 9, a sole arbitrator was appointed, and in due course the arbitrator awarded Skipper Rs 8.15 crore with interest — accepting, critically, that Flowmore had waived prototype inspection and that Skipper had legitimately manufactured and then sold the material as scrap. Flowmore\'s counter-claim of Rs 26.45 crore was dismissed.</p>

<h2>A procedural journey worth noting</h2>
<p>The award survived a Section 34 challenge before a Single Judge. An earlier Division Bench, rather than deciding the Section 37 appeal, remanded the Section 34 petition — and the Supreme Court set that remand aside in December 2024 with the pointed observation that there was absolutely no reason for the Division Bench to remand, restoring the appeal for adjudication on merits. The present judgment, reserved in September and pronounced on 28 October 2026, is the merits decision the Supreme Court directed.</p>

<h2>The three defects</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Waiver without evidence.</strong> The arbitrator\'s finding that Flowmore waived prototype inspection of the towers rested on an email of 19 June 2019 whose language, the Bench held, was clear: it gave a go-ahead for mass production of stubs and templates only. There was no evidence to substantiate waiver of prototype inspection of the towers themselves.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Internal contradiction.</strong> The waiver finding could not be reconciled with the arbitrator\'s own finding on when the advance became payable — the award was self-contradictory on its central logic.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Vital evidence ignored.</strong> Skipper\'s own inconsistent stance on whether the goods were customised went unconsidered, undermining the arbitrator\'s acceptance of the scrap sale as a legitimate mitigation step.</div>
</div>

<h2>The standard the Bench applied</h2>
<p>What gives the judgment its doctrinal value is its care in policing its own limits. The Bench acknowledged that a court under Sections 34 and 37 cannot re-appreciate evidence merely because another view is possible. The intervention rested instead on the recognised exceptions within patent illegality: a finding based on no evidence, a finding arrived at by ignoring vital evidence, and an award whose reasoning contradicts itself. On the no-evidence standard the Bench quoted the governing formulation — "no evidence" means truly no relevant evidence, not scant or weak evidence — before concluding that the waiver finding failed even that exacting test. The award was set aside and the appeal allowed.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>For award-holders, the case is a caution about awards that rest a chain of conclusions on a single document: if the document does not say what the award needs it to say, the entire chain is exposed. For challengers, it maps the only reliable route past Section 37 deference — not a better reading of the evidence, but a demonstration that a dispositive finding has nothing under it.</p>
</div>

<h2>Where this leaves the parties</h2>
<p>The setting aside of an award under Section 34 does not decree the counter-claim or decide the dispute; it removes the award, leaving the parties to their remedies, including fresh arbitration where available. Seven years after a cancelled purchase order, the parties are, in substance, back at the beginning — an outcome that itself carries a transactional lesson: contracts that condition advances, guarantees and manufacturing clearances on one another need equally precise correspondence during performance, because years later an arbitrator and three courts will parse a single go-ahead email word by word.</p>
